From c7a060d44832ab24d64b19202d92a13e71a4c056 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: James Morris <jmorris@namei.org>
Date: Thu, 5 Nov 2026 03:03:05 -0800
Subject: [PATCH 5/5] mvp: CHERI proof of concept: a soft-float userland, and
 M7's shell sessions

Four changes, validated together on the CHERI QEMU with beryllium-cheri's
two local changes (experiments/build-qemu-vs-reset.sh --ia-fix) and the
CHERI Alliance OpenSBI:

- The guest userland is soft-float, as Beryllium's is. The CHERI Alliance
  SDK's purecap musl and compiler-rt are hard-float (l64pc128d), and the
  guests have no FPU, so BusyBox's shell took an illegal instruction in
  setjmp. tools/build-guest-cheri.sh now builds compiler-rt's builtins
  and CRT objects and CHERI Alliance musl for l64pc128 from their pinned
  sources (CHERI_LLVM_SRC and CHERI_LLVM_COMMIT, CHERI_MUSL_SRC and
  CHERI_MUSL_COMMIT, which guest.mk passes and tools/make-control.py
  allows), and the init and BusyBox against them. guest/pins.txt is
  re-pinned; two builds give the same bytes.
- The init's vDSO workarounds go. The fault behind them (0f161828) is
  the CHERI QEMU's, not the kernel's: its invalid address conversion on
  a capability write to sepcc checks an Sv39 address against bit 37, not
  bit 38, and reads the mode from vsatp, so only under a hypervisor does
  the guest kernel's sepcc write before its sret turn a vDSO pc
  (0x3f8c623530) into an invalid one (0xffffffff8c623530). Natively, the
  same kernel and binaries return from the same fault correctly.
  beryllium-cheri's --ia-fix corrects the bit. touch_vdso and the
  clock_gettime system call are removed, so the init uses musl's
  clock_gettime. The ucontext offset's comment now names its cause: the
  guard for CHERI musl's smaller sigset_t tests __CHERI_PURE_CAPABILITY,
  which the compiler does not define.
- M7's shell sessions run: tests/session-typist.py types on the plic
  machine under BE_MVP_QEMU_K3_LIKE=plic and judges with the firmware's
  SBI 3.0.
- The monitor refuses at arming a hart that cannot delegate cause 28
  (BE_MVP_HEDELEG_REQUIRED 0x1000b10c), as tests/check-capture.py
  already required, since a CHERI guest takes its own CHERI faults.
  tests/sbi-test.c follows the CHERI monitor: hedeleg with cause 28,
  henvcfg STCE and CRE, B6's 0xb15d refused, and STCE alone refused;
  two more assertions (the Makefile's pin, 16294).

Results: make image references (BE_MVP_GUEST_BUILD: PASS cheri
pins=match), image-check (127, 131, and 140 mutations), reference-check,
port-check, lint, policy-negatives, guest-self-test, host-test (eleven
suites, GCC equal to Clang), target-check, budgets, and tool-lock-check
pass; tests/run-qemu.sh: BE_MVP_QEMU: PASS runs=48; and
tests/session-typist.py: BE_MVP_SESSIONS: PASS sessions=6.

Co-authored-by: Copilot <223556219+Copilot@users.noreply.github.com>
Copilot-Session: a7fa2f95-839f-4f32-a6fc-7415e24f2dbc
